  <p>
    The continuity theory, long the darling of Romanian national historiography, posits that the Romanized Dacians remained in situ after Rome’s legions departed in the 3rd century AD. According to this view, these hardy locals weathered the storms of barbarian invasions, preserving their Latin tongue and customs in the face of adversity. Linguistic evidence is often marshaled in support: Romanian is a Romance language, after all, and its vocabulary and grammar bear the unmistakable stamp of Latin. Archaeological finds—settlements, inscriptions, and artifacts—are cited as further proof that Roman culture did not vanish overnight. This narrative, neat and uplifting, has obvious appeal for a nation seeking ancient roots and a sense of continuity with the classical world.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yet, not all scholars are convinced. The migrationist or discontinuity theory suggests that the Romanized population largely evacuated Dacia when the empire pulled out, retreating south of the Danube. In this telling, the region was subsequently repopulated by a mélange of Slavs, Bulgars, and other migratory peoples, with the Romanian language and identity forming later, perhaps through a return migration from the Balkans. Proponents point to the relative scarcity of clear archaeological evidence for continuous settlement and the tumultuous nature of the so-called “Dark Ages” in the region. The discontinuity theory, while less romantic, cautions against reading modern identities too far back into the past.
