  </p>
  <p>
    Of course, no discussion of Romanian origins would be complete without acknowledging the political motivations that have colored the debate. In the 19th and 20th centuries, as Romania sought to assert itself on the European stage, the continuity theory became a cornerstone of national identity. Claiming descent from Rome was not just a matter of pride; it was a political tool, used to justify territorial claims and to distinguish Romanians from their Slavic and Hungarian neighbors. Conversely, rival states and some foreign historians have sometimes favored discontinuity theories, often for their own strategic reasons. In this way, the search for origins has often been less about the past than about the present.
  </p>
  <p>
