**My Answer:**

**Problem Restatement:**
Given n houses, each with a price, and m existing roads, we need to add the minimum-cost set of new roads so that the city is fully connected (i.e., there is a path between any two houses). The cost of a new road between house i and house j is a[i] + a[j].

**Approach:**
1. Use Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union, DSU) to find the connected components of the current graph.
2. For each component, find the house with the minimum price.
3. To connect all components with minimal cost, connect the component with the overall minimum-price house to the minimum-price house in every other component. This is optimal because connecting two components via their cheapest nodes minimizes the cost for that edge.
4. The number of new roads needed is (number of components - 1).
5. Output the new roads.

**Implementation (C++):**
```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct DSU {
    vector<int> parent;
    DSU(int n) : parent(n) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }
    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }
    void unite(int x, int y) {
        parent[find(x)] = find(y);
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> a[i];

    DSU dsu(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        --u; --v;
        dsu.unite(u, v);
    }

    // Find the minimum price house in each component
    unordered_map<int, pair<long long, int>> comp_min;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int root = dsu.find(i);
        if (comp_min.find(root) == comp_min.end() || a[i] < comp_min[root].first)
            comp_min[root] = {a[i], i};
    }

    // Find the component with the overall minimum price house
    int min_comp = -1, min_node = -1;
    long long min_price = LLONG_MAX;
    for (auto& [root, p] : comp_min) {
        if (p.first < min_price) {
            min_price = p.first;
            min_node = p.second;
            min_comp = root;
        }
    }

    // Connect all other components to the min_node
    vector<pair<int, int>> new_roads;
    for (auto& [root, p] : comp_min) {
        if (root == min_comp) continue;
        new_roads.emplace_back(min_node, p.second);
    }

    cout << new_roads.size() << "\n";
    for (auto& [u, v] : new_roads)
        cout << u + 1 << " " << v + 1 << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation:**
- We use DSU to find components.
- For each component, we find its cheapest house.
